>> >
>> > First off, try the very latest alsa-driver snapshot.
>> > =C2=A0ftp://ftp.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/people/tiwai/snapshot/alsa=
-driver-snapshot.tar.gz
>> >
>> > The problem is that BIOS sets only one stereo speaker in the pin
>> > configuration, so the driver cannot know what to do with another
>> > speaker. =C2=A0In short, it's a BIOS bug.
>> >
>> > To overcome that, you need to figure out which pin corresponds to the
>> > speaker. =C2=A0The rest available (analog) output pins are 0x0f and 0x=
14.
>> > Set these as output pins, and connect to DAC. =C2=A0For example,
>> >
>> > =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0# hda-verb /dev/snd/hwC0D0 0x0f SET_PIN_WID=
 0x40
>> > =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0# hda-verb /dev/snd/hwC0D0 0x0f SET_CONN 0
>> >
>> > which enables the output of the pin 0x0f, then connects to the first
>> > DAC (=3D 0x10). =C2=A0Similarly,
>> >
>> > =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0# hda-verb /dev/snd/hwC0D0 0x14 SET_PIN_WID=
 0x40
>> > =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0# hda-verb /dev/snd/hwC0D0 0x15 SET_CONN 0
>> >
>> > which enables the output of the pin 0x14, then changes the connection
>> > of its mux (0x15) to the first DAC (0x10).
>> >
>> > If these don't help, it's possible that some GPIO bits play any role.
>> > Play with GPIO bits, which can be also changed via hda-verb.
>> >
>> >
>> > Takashi
